.Dd July 20, 2026
.Dt LIBSERVICE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm service_init ,
.Nm service_ready ,
.Nm service_register ,
.Nm service_unregister ,
.Nm service_lookup ,
.Nm service_accept ,
.Nm service_send ,
.Nm service_recv ,
.Nm service_channel_fd ,
.Nm service_capability_fd ,
.Nm service_authorize_capabilities ,
.Nm service_protect
.Nd client library for services managed by serviced
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libservice
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In libservice.h
.Ft int
.Fn service_init void
.Ft int
.Fn service_ready void
.Ft int
.Fn service_register "const char *name"
.Ft int
.Fn service_unregister "const char *name"
.Ft int
.Fn service_lookup "const char *name"
.Ft int
.Fn service_accept "char *client_label" "size_t labelsz"
.Ft int
.Fn service_send "int fd" "const void *data" "size_t len"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn service_recv "int fd" "void *buf" "size_t bufsz" "int *peer_fd"
.Ft int
.Fn service_channel_fd void
.Ft int
.Fn service_capability_fd "const char *name"
.Ft int
.Fn service_authorize_capabilities void
.Ft int
.Fn service_protect "uint32_t flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm libservice
library provides a clean API for services managed by
.Xr serviced 8
to communicate with the service manager over their inherited
mac_capability pair channel.
Services link against
.Nm libservice
for lifecycle and transport operations.
Programs using a named capability service include that service's protocol
header and issue its service-specific ioctl on the descriptor returned by
.Fn service_capability_fd .
.Pp
.Fn service_init
initializes the library by reading the
.Ev ORACLED_CHANNEL_FD
environment variable to obtain the pair file descriptor
inherited from
.Xr serviced 8 .
This function must be called before any other function in the
library.
.Pp
.Fn service_ready
reports readiness to
.Xr serviced 8 .
This notifies the service manager that the service has completed
initialization and is ready to serve requests.
.Pp
.Fn service_register
registers a reverse-domain name
.Fa name
(e.g.,
.Dq org.5bsd.sshd )
with the
.Xr serviced 8
naming registry.
After registration, other services can connect via
.Fn service_lookup .
The name must contain at least one dot, consist only of
alphanumerics, dots, hyphens, and underscores, and must not
exceed 255 characters.
.Pp
.Fn service_unregister
removes a previously registered name from the naming registry.
Only the service that registered the name may remove it.
.Pp
.Fn service_lookup
connects to a named service by requesting a brokered connection
from
.Xr serviced 8 .
The service manager creates a new mac_capability pair, pushes one end
to the provider and returns the other end to the caller.
On success, a file descriptor for the bidirectional pair endpoint
is returned.
.Pp
.Fn service_accept
blocks until a client connects to one of this service's
registered names via
.Fn service_lookup .
On success, a file descriptor for the client connection is
returned.
If
.Fa client_label
is not
.Dv NULL ,
the connecting service's label is copied into it, up to
.Fa labelsz
bytes.
Notifications that arrive during an in-progress RPC are queued
internally and delivered from subsequent calls to
.Fn service_accept .
.Pp
.Fn service_send
sends a message on an arbitrary mac_capability pair file descriptor
.Fa fd .
This is a convenience wrapper around the
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_SENDMSG
ioctl.
.Pp
.Fn service_recv
receives a message from a mac_capability pair file descriptor
.Fa fd .
Up to
.Fa bufsz
bytes are read into
.Fa buf .
If
.Fa peer_fd
is not
.Dv NULL
and the message carries an attached file descriptor, it is
stored in
.Fa *peer_fd ;
otherwise
.Fa *peer_fd
is set to \-1.
This is a convenience wrapper around the
.Dv MAC_CAPABILITY_RECVMSG
ioctl.
.Pp
.Fn service_channel_fd
returns the pair file descriptor to
.Xr serviced 8
that was established during
.Fn service_init .
This is useful for registering the fd with
.Xr kqueue 2
for event-driven services.
Returns \-1 if the library has not been initialized.
.Pp
.Fn service_capability_fd
returns the borrowed descriptor associated with a manifest-requested
.Dq mount ,
.Dq node ,
.Dq accounting ,
or
.Dq identity
capability service.
The mapping from
.Ev ORACLED_CAPABILITY_FDS
is parsed during
.Fn service_init ,
and every descriptor is verified against its kernel-reported service name.
These are authority-bearing service descriptors and require no token
activation.
.Pp
.Fn service_authorize_capabilities
activates every isolation token descriptor named by the inherited
.Ev ORACLED_TOKEN_FDS
environment variable for the calling process.
.Xr serviced 8
delivers tokens but never activates them; the service must explicitly call
this function after
.Xr execve 2
when it chooses to accept the manifest authorities.
The complete descriptor list is parsed and every descriptor is verified as an
isolation or system token before any token is activated.
After validation, private duplication must also succeed before activation
begins; a duplication failure leaves the caller-visible state unchanged.
Once activation begins, the handles are consumed: the listed descriptor
numbers are closed and
.Ev ORACLED_TOKEN_FDS
is removed from the environment, whether activation succeeds completely or
one of the activation operations fails.
On complete success the library retains private close-on-exec duplicates for
the process lifetime, because closing the last token reference revokes the
kernel authorization.
The original descriptor numbers must not be reused by the caller.
Calling the function again after successful activation is therefore a
no-op.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn service_init ,
.Fn service_authorize_capabilities ,
.Fn service_protect ,
.Fn service_ready ,
.Fn service_register ,
.Fn service_unregister ,
and
.Fn service_send
return 0 on success and \-1 on failure, setting
.Va errno .
.Pp
.Fn service_lookup
and
.Fn service_accept
return a non-negative file descriptor on success and \-1 on
failure, setting
.Va errno .
.Pp
.Fn service_recv
returns the number of bytes received on success and \-1 on
failure, setting
.Va errno .
.Pp
.Fn service_channel_fd
returns the pair file descriptor, or \-1 if the library has not
been initialized.
.Fn service_capability_fd
returns a non-negative borrowed descriptor on success and \-1 on failure.
.Pp
.Fn service_protect
applies
.Xr mac_capability_capprotect 4
shielding to the calling service using the
.Ev ORACLED_CAPPROTECT_FD
descriptor inherited from
.Xr serviced 8 .
The
.Fa flags
argument is a
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_*
bitmask.
The external-interference flags are
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_PTRACE ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_SIGNAL ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_VISIBLE ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_WAIT ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_SIGKILL ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_SIGCONT ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_SCHED ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_CORE ,
and
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_KTRACE .
The self-restriction flags are
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_NOPRIVS ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_NOFORK ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_NOIPC ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_NOFDRECV ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_NOEXEC ,
and
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_NOSOCK .
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_EXTERNAL ,
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_RESTRICT ,
and
.Dv SERVICE_PROTECT_ALL
provide the corresponding aggregate masks.
A flags value of 0 requests every protection known to the running kernel;
an explicit mask gives the service a stable, reviewable policy.
It returns 0 on success, or \-1 on failure with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Basic provider pattern:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <libservice.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    char label[64];
    int client_fd;

    if (service_init() == -1)
        return (1);

    if (service_authorize_capabilities() == -1)
        return (1);
    if (service_protect(SERVICE_PROTECT_EXTERNAL) == -1)
        return (1);
    if (service_register("org.example.myservice") == -1)
        return (1);
    if (service_ready() == -1)
        return (1);

    for (;;) {
        client_fd = service_accept(label, sizeof(label));
        if (client_fd == -1)
            break;
        printf("client connected: %s\\n", label);
        /* handle client_fd ... */
    }

    service_unregister("org.example.myservice");
    return (0);
}
.Ed
.Pp
Basic client pattern:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <libservice.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    int peer;
    char buf[256];
    ssize_t n;

    if (service_init() == -1)
        return (1);

    service_ready();

    peer = service_lookup("org.example.myservice");
    if (peer == -1)
        return (1);

    service_send(peer, "hello", 5);
    n = service_recv(peer, buf, sizeof(buf), NULL);
    if (n > 0)
        printf("received %zd bytes\\n", n);

    return (0);
}
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
The following errors are common to multiple functions:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOTCONN
The library has not been initialized via
.Fn service_init ,
or the pair fd has become invalid.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
An invalid argument was passed (e.g., a malformed name).
.El
.Pp
.Fn service_init
may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The
.Ev ORACLED_CHANNEL_FD
environment variable is not set.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The value of
.Ev ORACLED_CHANNEL_FD
is not a valid descriptor number or does not identify a
.Nm mac_capability
channel endpoint, or
.Ev ORACLED_CAPPROTECT_FD
is present but does not identify a capprotect instance, or
.Ev ORACLED_CAPABILITY_FDS
is malformed or names a descriptor of the wrong capability-service type.
.El
.Pp
.Fn service_register
may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The name exceeds 255 characters.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
The name is already registered by another service.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
The service has reached its per-service registration limit (32).
.El
.Pp
.Fn service_unregister
may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The name exceeds 255 characters.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The name is not registered.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The name is registered by a different service.
.El
.Pp
.Fn service_lookup
may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The name exceeds 255 characters.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The name is not registered.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
The caller attempted to connect to itself.
.It Bq Er ECONNRESET
The provider's pair channel has closed.
.It Bq Er EDEADLK
An on-demand circular dependency was detected
(the provider requires a service that the caller provides).
.It Bq Er ETIMEDOUT
The lookup RPC did not complete within the internal timeout
(100 ms).
This can occur if the provider is being launched on-demand
and has not yet registered.
.It Bq Er EIO
Failed to create a pair or notify the provider.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr mac_capability_capprotect 4 ,
.Xr serviced 5 ,
.Xr serviced 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm libservice
first appeared in 5BSD.
.Sh CAVEATS
The library is not thread-safe.
The internal pair fd and notification queue are stored in static
variables; concurrent access from multiple threads will produce
undefined behavior.
.Pp
The library is not safe to use across
.Xr fork 2
without a subsequent
.Xr execve 2 .
The pair fd is shared between parent and child after fork, and
interleaved messages will corrupt protocol state.
.Pp
Descriptors returned by
.Fn service_lookup
and
.Fn service_accept
arrive with
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE .
They can be used for communication but cannot be delegated again.
.Pp
.Fn service_accept
blocks on the pair fd.
If the service needs to multiplex the pair fd with other event
sources, use
.Fn service_channel_fd
to obtain the fd and register it with
.Xr kqueue 2
or
.Xr poll 2 ,
then call
.Fn service_accept
only when the fd is readable.
